      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to fix my lawn. it looks pretty yellow and thin in some spots. can you explain what those NPK numbers on fertilizer bags actually stand for? also what does it look like if the grass is missing phosphorus?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those NPK numbers on fertilizer bags represent the levels of three essential nutrients for plant growth: Nitrogen (N), Phosphorus (P), and Potassium (K). A balanced fertilizer typically provides a ratio of N:P:K, with the most common being 10-10-10 or 15-5-10. If the grass is missing phosphorus, it may exhibit signs such as slow growth, weak and spindly blades, or an uneven green color, especially on roots and crown areas (around the base of the plant).",
